   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-calculus-bc",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.3",
    "topic_title": "Estimating Limit Values from Graphs",
    "stem": "The graph of f is described as follows: As x approaches 3 from the left, the graph approaches an open circle at (3, 2). As x approaches 3 from the right, the graph approaches an open circle at (3, 6). A filled point is located at (3, 4). What is lim x→3 f(x)?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "2",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Left-side-only error: This uses the value approached by the left branch but ignores the right branch, which approaches 6. Fix: Trace both branches toward the input and compare their approached values."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "The limit does not exist.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The graph approaches 2 from the left and 6 from the right. A two-sided limit requires the same value from both sides, so the unequal one-sided limits mean this limit does not exist."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "6",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Right-side-only error: This uses the value approached by the right branch but ignores the left branch, which approaches 2. Fix: Check the left-hand behavior before concluding that a two-sided limit exists."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "4",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Function-value confusion: The filled point gives f(3) = 4, not the value approached nearby. The two branches approach different heights, so the filled point cannot determine this limit. Fix: Trace the nearby branches from both sides before looking at the filled point."
     }
    ]
   },
